**Theorem 88.1 (structure of a repeated-kernel multiplier).** Let $j,C,c,k$ be positive integers, let $N=3^j\ge81$, and suppose that $k$ is odd, $3\nmid k$, and

$$B_N=DC^2,\qquad B_{Nk}=Dc^2,$$

where $D$ is the full positive squarefree kernel of $B_N$. Then every prime $q\mid B_N$ has even valuation in $k$. Moreover,

$$k=s^2\quad\text{or}\quad k=rs^2,$$

where $s$ is a positive integer supported on the prime divisors of $B_N$, and, in the second case, $r$ is a prime not dividing $B_N$. If $s>1$ in the second case, $r$ exceeds every prime divisor of $s$.

If $k$ is composite and $p=P^-(k)$, then

$$p\mid B_N,\qquad v_p(k)\in2\mathbb Z_{>0},\qquad p\ge6N+1,\qquad Nk\ge N(6N+1)^2.$$

In particular, if $k>1$ and $\gcd(k,B_N)=1$, then $k$ is prime.

**Proof.** We first locate the least prime divisor of a composite multiplier, then prove the valuation identity and the two-step descent needed to remove its original-support square factors.

**The least prime divisor.** Suppose $k$ is composite and $p=P^-(k)$. Thus $p\ge5$. Set $t=Nk/p$. The Lucas-polynomial divisibility of §86 gives $B_N\mid B_t$, and $k\ge p^2$ gives $t\ge Np$. Since $N\ge81$, one has $t-1>40p$. The Binet estimate and the universal quotient bound of §87 therefore give

$$L_t>2^{(t-1)/2}>2^{20p},\qquad Q_p(L_t)\text{ is not a square},$$

where $Q_p(X)=(V_p(X)^2+3)/(X^2+3)$. Yet

$$B_{Nk}=B_tQ_p(L_t),\qquad \gcd(B_t,Q_p(L_t))\mid p.$$

If $p\nmid B_t$, the two factors are coprime. Every prime of $D$ divides $B_t$, so the repeated-kernel equation would force $Q_p(L_t)$ to be a square. Consequently $p\mid B_t$. The prime $5$ never divides $B_t$, since $-3$ is not a square modulo $5$, so $p>5$.

The Pell identity $5F_t^2=B_t+1$ makes $5$ a square modulo $p$. Choose $\varphi=(1+\sqrt5)/2$ in $\mathbb F_p$ and put $a=\varphi^2$. Then $a^t$ has order $3$. With $h=\operatorname{ord}(a)$,

$$\frac{h}{\gcd(h,t)}=3,\qquad h\mid p-1,\qquad v_3(h)=j+1.$$

Every prime divisor of $h$ other than $3$ divides $t/3^j=k/p$. Every prime divisor of $k/p$ is at least $p$, whereas $h\le p-1$. Thus $h=3^{j+1}=3N$. It follows that $a^N$ is a nontrivial cube root of unity, whence $p\mid B_N$. Since $p-1$ is even and $3N$ is odd, $6N\mid p-1$ and $p\ge6N+1$.

**Valuations on the full original support.** Let $q\mid B_N$. The original-support inputs TBN3 and GPC5 give $q>5$ and splitting of $5$ modulo $q$. Lift a square root of $5$ to $\mathbb Z_q$, and again put $\varphi=(1+\sqrt5)/2$ and $a=\varphi^2$. Modulo $q$, $a^N$ is a nontrivial cube root of unity. For every positive odd integer $m$ with $3\nmid m$,

$$B_{Nm}=\frac{a^{3Nm}-1}{a^{Nm}(a^{Nm}-1)}.$$

The denominator is a $q$-adic unit. Set $u=a^{3N}$. Then $u\equiv1\pmod q$, and $u\ne1$ because the algebraic unit $\varphi$ has infinite order and its embedding in $\mathbb Q_q$ is injective. Odd-prime lifting gives

$$v_q(u^m-1)=v_q(u-1)+v_q(m),$$

and hence

$$v_q(B_{Nm})=v_q(B_N)+v_q(m).\tag{88.1}$$

Here the lifting identity is elementary: write $m=q^em_0$ with $q\nmid m_0$. The geometric quotient for $u^{m_0}-1$ is congruent to $m_0$ modulo $q$. At each subsequent $q$-th-power step, the first binomial term $q(z-1)$ has valuation $1+v_q(z-1)$, strictly smaller than all remaining terms because $q$ is odd.

Equality of the full squarefree kernels of $B_N$ and $B_{Nk}$ makes their $q$-valuations have the same parity. Equation (88.1) therefore makes $v_q(k)$ even for every $q\mid B_N$. For the least prime $p$ of a composite $k$, this valuation is positive, so $k\ge p^2\ge(6N+1)^2$. This proves the asserted composite-index bound.

**Removing an original-support prime square.** Suppose $q\mid B_N$ and $q^2\mid k$. Put $t_1=Nk/q$ and $t_2=Nk/q^2$. Both are odd multiples of $N$ with relative multipliers coprime to $3$, so $B_N$ divides both $B_{t_1}$ and $B_{t_2}$. The quotient gcd and (88.1) give

$$\gcd(B_{t_1},Q_q(L_{t_1}))\mid q,\qquad v_q(Q_q(L_{t_1}))=1.$$

Every other prime in this quotient is absent from $B_{t_1}$, and hence absent from $D$. Since $B_{Nk}=B_{t_1}Q_q(L_{t_1})$ has squarefree kernel $D$, every such quotient prime has even valuation. Thus

$$Q_q(L_{t_1})=qu_1^2$$

for a positive integer $u_1$. For every prime already dividing $B_{t_1}$ other than $q$, its valuation is unchanged in the product. The squarefree kernel of $B_{t_1}$ is therefore precisely $D$ with the membership of $q$ toggled.

All primes of this toggled kernel still divide $B_N$, including $q$ even when $q\nmid D$ initially. They consequently divide $B_{t_2}$. Apply the same gcd and valuation argument to

$$B_{t_1}=B_{t_2}Q_q(L_{t_2}).$$

It gives $Q_q(L_{t_2})=qu_2^2$ for a positive integer $u_2$. The two toggles cancel, and $B_{t_2}$ again has full squarefree kernel $D$. Thus $B_{t_2}=Dc_2^2$ for a positive integer $c_2$. This is a two-step descent; a single step need not preserve $D$.

**The outside-support part.** Every original-support prime has even exponent in $k$. Repeatedly remove its square factors by the preceding descent. The resulting positive odd multiplier $k_0$ is coprime to $3B_N$ and still satisfies the same-kernel equation. If $k_0$ were composite, the least-prime argument would place its least prime divisor in $B_N$, a contradiction. Hence $k_0=1$ or $k_0=r$ for a prime $r\nmid B_N$. This proves the two stated forms of $k$.

Finally, suppose $r$ is present and some prime divisor of $s$ exceeds $r$. Remove every original-support factor below $r$ by the same two-step descent. The remaining multiplier is composite, has least prime divisor $r$, and still satisfies the same-kernel equation. The least-prime argument would force $r\mid B_N$, again a contradiction. Thus every prime divisor of $s$ is smaller than $r$. ∎

